#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba963ded-e9ff-5324-9d22-acb6b7a85e10" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "b13213ea-be0a-5f1a-85fd-02b69556bd58" :question/math-academy-id "e-3537" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$, what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3} &= 0 \\\\\n3\\cot x &= -3\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\cot x &= -\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n\\cot x &= -\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\arctan (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) =-30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-30^\\circ$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution. However, we can generate solutions by adding integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&=-30^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 150^{∘} \\\\\nx_{2} &= x + 360^{∘} \\\\\n&=-30^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\\\\n&= 330^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=150^\\circ, 330^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1 \\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n150 \\cdot 330 = 49\\,500\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114928 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c9c0f6-034c-5d9c-935a-ca27dec1a648" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "a30eea56-2088-549f-a986-08d54117615b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3539" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $-180^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ$, what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\cot x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3} &= 0 \\\\\n3\\cot x &= -3\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\cot x &= -\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n\\cot x &= -\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\arctan (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) =-30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-30^\\circ$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 =-30^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&=-30^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 150^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx= -30^\\circ, 150^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n-30\\cdot(150) = -4\\,500\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114970 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c71c49ee-e496-5d4e-87fa-96e28dd830ca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "bc14e14d-d58f-5556-af4d-4aa52f394e58" :question/math-academy-id "e-4266" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\cot x =9 - \\cot x$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$, what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$, rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cot x &= 9 - \\cot x \\\\\n3\\cot x &= 9 \\\\\n\\cot x &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = arctan(\\frac{1}{3}) = 0.321750 \\approx 0.3218\n$$\n\nSince $0.321\\,8$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0.321\\,8\n$$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + π \\\\\n&= π + 0.3218 \\\\\n&= 3.4634\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n0.321\\,8 \\cdot 3.463\\,4 \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115055 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation When the Cotangent Function Equals Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "9344fc13-add4-57bc-9558-73cc0b95fea0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7699" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3- 2\\cot x =3$ for $0^\\circ < x < 360^\\circ$, what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 - 2\\cot x &= 3 \\\\\n- 2\\cot x &= 0 \\\\\n\\cot x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0$:\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{1}{0} = \\textrm{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\cos x &=0 \\\\[5pt] x &= \\arccos 0 \\\\[5pt] x &= 90^\\circ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 90^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 270^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are\n\n$$\nx=90^\\circ, 270^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n90 \\cdot(270) = 24\\,300\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c71c49ee-e496-5d4e-87fa-96e28dd830ca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8497b40f-7dd9-57c9-af8d-f64edaad0187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3916" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113163} {:answer/id #uuid "da02af14-b5d9-5bf8-9e2b-9a9a8ac88d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3996" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113164} {:answer/id #uuid "09de61be-4034-56e7-8458-c54c20a6bec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3956" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113165} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3da09a-6e79-546f-b942-a84d539a98a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3936" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113166} {:answer/id #uuid "7669088c-2bab-5c78-948c-a04c784acba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3976" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da02af14-b5d9-5bf8-9e2b-9a9a8ac88d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3996" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113164} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a5db227-9bc7-553a-9332-6fe7b9b26899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113162}] :question/id #uuid "2a1aca94-95fd-5220-bcc5-e0d2edb4ba00" :question/math-academy-id "q-29242" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $5\\cot x = 11 + \\cot x$ for $0^{∘} \\le x < 360^{∘}$, what is the numerical value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
